\section{Introduction}
%emw9Aug2025: Good stuff for a cover letter from below: These phenological differences among taxa have often been overlooked in ecological theory, but growing evidence suggests that they may play a major role in dictating community interactions and shaping the structure and function of ecological communities \citep{Cleland2024,Blackford_2020,volkerisaj}. 
Each spring, in ecosystems across the temperate zone, ecological communities re-assemble from their period of winter dormancy with the hatching of insects, arrival of migratory birds, and emergence of new seedlings, leaves and flowers. The seasonal timing of these life cycle events---their phenology---can be quite different, even among closely related organisms in a shared habitat \citep{Ovaskainen2013,An2022}. These phenological differences among taxa have often been overlooked in ecological theory outside of plant-pollinator interactions, but growing evidence suggests that they may play a major role in dictating community interactions and shaping the structure and function of ecological communities \citep{Cleland2024,Blackford_2020,volkerisaj}. 

