\section{Paths Forward}
On the experimental side, more studies that quantify species-level phenological sensitivity could help answer open questions in ecophysiology with major implications for forecasting. Studies that estimate the phenological sensitivity to multiple environmental cues could address how much local adaptation versus plasticity structure phenological sensitivity and competitive traits (e.g. \citep{Lipowsky:2015aa,Liang:2021aa}, but will be most useful for forecasting if they include multiple environmental cues and a greater scope of taxa and lifeforms. Estimates of species-level phenological sensitivity, quantified at multiple scales (i.e., population, individual) would allow researchers to parameterize community assembly models for species of interest. This could form the start of practical application of these models, building the groundwork for modelers and experimentalists work together to build predictive tools that aid ecological practitioners. But this will require expanding the study systems and flexibility of current models by both research groups. 

Models of ecological competition that include phenology are often developed to encapsulate competition between two annual plants \citep{godoy2014,wolkovich2021phenological,levine2022competition}. However, herbaceous perennials and woody plants are the dominant functional types in many ecological systems, and, as such, phenology-mediated competition occurs between individuals both within- (i.e., seedling vs. seedling or mature plant vs mature plant) and across- (i.e, mature plant vs. seedling) life stages. While studies in woody plants suggest separate phenological phases can respond quite differently to the same environmental cues \citep{Buonaiuto:2021aa} and that juveniles and adults express substantially different phenological patterns in the same environments \citep{Vitasse:2013aa}, changes in phenological sensitivity to multiple cues across multiple life stages relevant to community assembly (i.e., seed germination vs vegetative cues) have not been widely explored across taxa. To help, modelers can construct more biologically realistic community assembly models that include multiple growth forms and life stages. At the same time, experimentalists could design studies that explore how environmental variability influences phenological differences across life-stages and mediates both intra- an inter-specific competition within and across these stages.

%emw9Aug2025: There is a lot of passive voice in this paragraph! I took some out but watch out. 
On the modeling side, researchers should integrate the more complex cue responses to assembly models. Current models---including those conceptualized for seed germination---include phenology independent of or weakly linked to the environment, but research has repeatedly shown than phenology is determined to multiple, interacting environmental cues. For seed germination this often includes multiple aspects of temperature (i.e., forcing and chilling), light (daylength or irradiance) and moisture \citep{Baskin2014,Forrest2010}, but models to date have ignored this. Selection for interacting cues is generally thought to be driven by another component likely critical for better predictions---environmental risk.

Complex cues can reduce risk \citep{Bonamour:2019aa} but they also result in a more conservative phenological strategy. For example, leafout of many forest trees relies on cues of winter chilling and/or photoperiod, which could reduce the risk of frost damage in unstable spring environments, but generally also results a later phenological transitions \citep{Zohner:2017aa}. Ecological models generally subsume such risks into species traits (e.g., increasing maintenance costs), which misses the reality that such risks are also product of timing $\times$ environment. 

A changing risk landscape associated with a changing environment may be the underlying driver of observed correlations between the timing and intensity component of life cycle transitions \citep[i.e., germination phenology and total germination percent of a seed cohort][]{Baskin2014}. But models will need to explicitly include the environment and phenology to predict this. Further, for plants---and likely other organisms---the timing and fraction of phenological success (e.g., seed germination or leafout) usually covary in nature, which likely has important implications for coexistence. Building models that can isolate these two trade-off axes of coexistence---timing and abundance (fraction)---would allow offer to assess the relative importance of these within- and across season trade-offs.

Another dimension of risk and phenology could help generate more predictive assembly models is to understand how another aspect of phenology that is sensitive to environmental cues---the duration of a phenological phases---mitigates or exacerbates environmental risk. Environmental cues not only advance or delay the start of a phenological transition, but may also influence the rate of the transition. More protracted phenological phases (i.e., if the germination of a full seed cohort takes longer) associated with unfavorable conditions may serve as a form of within-season bet-hedging in which an untimely mortality event would have a lower fitness cost to a cohort of seed that started germinating early but slowly (at the cohort level) than one that started early and finished fast. Introducing biologically realistic mortality events into community assembly models that represent important selective forces, such as late-season frosts or early droughts, offers a straightforward approach to assess the importance of the often overlooked aspect of phenology. %emw9Aug2025:  Could cite some eco-evo climate change papers here?

The modeling framework we present has the flexibility to incorporate many of the ecological aspects we discussed above (multiple cues, environmental risk, within- and across- season bet-hedging). But making these predictions meaningful will require modelers and physiologists working together to further develop models with biologically accurate physiological processes, and experiments that can readily  inform model parameters and development. In a rapidly changing world, these kind of cross-disciplinary collaborations are critical to translate ecological theory into practice and advance a more predictive ecology than is better equipped to inform ecosystem conservation and management.


